---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
---

<BaseLayout title="About">
  <main class="py-24 bg-brand-900 text-brand-200">
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 flex flex-col gap-12">
      <header class="flex flex-col gap-4">
        <h1 class="font-heading text-5xl sm:text-6xl font-extrabold uppercase text-brand-50">About This Demo</h1>
        <p class="text-lg text-brand-300 leading-relaxed">
          Form First is a guided multi‑step booking flow built as a front‑end case study. It focuses on clarity, structure, and
          component‑driven UI — showing how small businesses can offer a simple, trustworthy way for clients to book sessions.
        </p>
      </header>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl font-semibold text-brand-50 uppercase">Purpose</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          This project was created to demonstrate how a clean booking experience can be designed and built without the weight of a full
          product. It highlights practical front‑end skills: multi‑step flows, state management, form design, and a calm, predictable user
          journey.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl font-semibold text-brand-50 uppercase">What It Includes</h2>

        <ul class="space-y-4">
          <li class="border-l-4 border-primary pl-4 py-2">
            <strong class="text-brand-50">Multi‑step booking flow</strong><br />
            Service selection, date/time picking, personal details, review, and confirmation.
          </li>

          <li class="border-l-4 border-secondary pl-4 py-2">
            <strong class="text-brand-50">Component‑driven structure</strong><br />
            Each step is isolated, reusable, and built with clarity in mind.
          </li>

          <li class="border-l-4 border-primary pl-4 py-2">
            <strong class="text-brand-50">Mocked “Manage Booking” view</strong><br />
            A simple interface showing how a user might review or cancel a booking.
          </li>

          <li class="border-l-4 border-secondary pl-4 py-2">
            <strong class="text-brand-50">Mocked admin list</strong><br />
            A lightweight admin-style page that reads from local JSON to simulate real data.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl font-semibold text-brand-50 uppercase">What’s Not Included</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          This demo intentionally avoids backend complexity. There is no authentication, no database, and no real scheduling logic. All data
          is mocked to keep the focus on front‑end flow, not infrastructure.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="flex flex-col gap-6">
        <h2 class="font-heading text-3xl font-semibold text-brand-50 uppercase">If This Were a Real Client Project</h2>
        <p class="leading-relaxed">
          A production version would connect to a backend service (such as Supabase, Airtable, or a custom API) to store bookings, send
          notifications, and manage availability. The front‑end flow would remain largely the same — only the data layer would change.
        </p>
      </section>

      <footer class="pt-12 border-t border-brand-800">
        <p class="text-brand-400 text-sm">Form First is a demo project built with the Pennine Design System.</p>
      </footer>
    </div>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
